WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.270
Nobody knows what this object is.

2
00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:04.325
It was photographed from the space station,

3
00:00:04.325 --> 00:00:06.959
one of countless bits of orbiting junk.

4
00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:09.228
Space debris is anything up there that

5
00:00:09.228 --> 00:00:10.714
no longer works:

6
00:00:10.714 --> 00:00:12.769
dead satellites, spent rocket stages,

7
00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:14.997
fragments, even flecks of paint.

8
00:00:15.396 --> 00:00:18.159
The US Space Surveillance Network has catalogued

9
00:00:18.159 --> 00:00:20.504
more than thirty-four thousand objects.

10
00:00:20.504 --> 00:00:23.105
It tracks pieces bigger than about ten

11
00:00:23.105 --> 00:00:26.563
centimetres; smaller ones mostly go unseen.

12
00:00:26.963 --> 00:00:28.496
In low orbit,

13
00:00:28.496 --> 00:00:30.725
debris moves at seven to eight kilometres

14
00:00:30.725 --> 00:00:31.677
a second.

15
00:00:31.677 --> 00:00:34.730
Crashes happen at about ten on average,

16
00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:37.155
so even a tiny piece hits hard.

17
00:00:37.555 --> 00:00:39.726
These pits were made by tiny particles,

18
00:00:39.726 --> 00:00:41.212
on a panel that NASA brought back

19
00:00:41.212 --> 00:00:42.360
from orbit.

20
00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.500
Two events made it much worse:

21
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.613
a satellite destroyed on purpose in two

22
00:00:47.613 --> 00:00:49.006
thousand and seven,

23
00:00:49.006 --> 00:00:51.340
and an accidental crash in two thousand

24
00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:52.302
and nine.

25
00:00:52.701 --> 00:00:54.640
The space station dodges when the risk

26
00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:55.940
of a hit tops one in ten

27
00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.287
thousand.

28
00:00:57.287 --> 00:00:58.878
Below six hundred kilometres,

29
00:00:58.878 --> 00:01:00.942
debris falls back within years.

30
00:01:01.342 --> 00:01:02.503
For GS Three,

31
00:01:02.503 --> 00:01:04.918
link this to space sustainability:

32
00:01:04.918 --> 00:01:07.054
where dead hardware is left decides how

33
00:01:07.054 --> 00:01:08.376
long it stays.

